# Dispatching agents through dispatch.py and herdr

This skill holds everything an agent needs to drive `dispatch.py` and `herdr`
correctly on Bedrock. It is the short form of
`.claude/skills/codex-dispatch/dispatch.py` (read the file for the measurement
behind each refusal) plus the operational knowledge that is nowhere written:
the ten mistakes made on 2026-08-14, and the rules that bind every dispatch.

Every operational claim here is marked MEASURED, INFERRED or UNMEASURED.
MEASURED means it was observed on this machine, with the date. INFERRED means
it follows from the code without a live run. UNMEASURED means nobody has
observed it and the skill does not recommend it.

## The trigger

This skill loads itself from the model side. A model matches the
`description:` field above when it is about to dispatch an agent, queue or
resume one, stop or kill one, read a dispatch status, arm or re-arm a waiter,
pick a head or a model, write a `tier:` line, or touch a herdr pane, agent
name, or send-keys. There is no slash command and no magic word.

## The one command: read the policy, never recall it

`scripts/dispatch_policy.py` is the ONE home of DD17's tables (DD19: a head
restated anywhere else is canonical twice). `[LJ-1.183]` found the tables
restated in `dev/ORCHESTRATION.md` this week and it is gone. Run:

```sh
python3 scripts/dispatch_policy.py
```

It prints: the version in force, when it was set, by whom, why, the condition
that reverts it, the full table for all three cases, the invariant, and the
default harness `dispatch.py` picks. Never answer "which head runs this task"
from memory.

## The two modes, both tables

Since 2026-08-13 DD17 has two versions and exactly one is in force. Each is
named for the head it LEADS with (owner, 2026-08-14; the retired names
`normal` and `override` said which one was the EXCEPTION and never which head
leads).

| case | deepseek-subagent-mode | in-harness-subagent-mode |
|---|---|---|
| default | herdr / pi / tier `pi` | in-harness / opus 5 / tier `opus` |
| adversarial | in-harness / opus 5 / tier `opus` | herdr / pi / tier `pi` |
| fallback | herdr / codex / tier `codex` | herdr / codex / tier `codex` |

The two tables swap exactly the default row and the adversarial row. The
invariant under both: **the critic is never the same head as the author.**

The retired names still RESOLVE, through `ALIASES` in the same file: `normal`
maps to `deepseek-subagent-mode`, `override` maps to
`in-harness-subagent-mode`. 52 frozen briefs carry `override`; a brief is a
record and a record is never rewritten, so the old names resolve forever
(C-41).

## The model rule, ruled 2026-08-14 and separate from the head

`model_for()` in the same file, and it is nearly mechanical:

- A task that is pure natural-language work and touches no Agda code takes
  `deepseek-v4-flash`.
- Everything else, meaning any task that runs Agda, takes `deepseek-v4-pro`.

It hangs on the existing `--agda` flag, so it costs no new declaration.
What it CANNOT see: a task that runs no Agda but READS and reasons about Agda
source. `--agda` is about holding a process, not about subject matter, so
the orchestrator overrides with `--model` and the brief records why.

**THE MODEL IS A COMMAND-LINE FLAG, AND THE BRIEF DOES NOT CHANGE IT.**
`dispatch.py` never reads the model from the brief's `tier:` line; the
default is `deepseek-v4-pro` and the line exists for the audit. A brief
that names a non-default model is honored only when the dispatch command
passes `--model <that model>`. MEASURED 2026-08-14: the first LJ-1.187
launch ran `deepseek-v4-pro` (its launch log's agent-start `argv`) while
its brief names flash; the tier line records the intended choice and the
command line makes it real.

The model name has NO date suffix. The API rejects `deepseek-v4-pro-0813`
and accepts only `deepseek-v4-pro` or `deepseek-v4-flash` (MEASURED
2026-08-13). `dispatch.py` refuses a model the backend rejects; two agents
died that way before the refusal existed.

## The tier: line

A brief's `tier:` line names the head AND the mode, and the model when it is
not the default. Form: `tier: <head> (<mode>), model <model>`. Example:

```markdown
tier: pi (deepseek-subagent-mode), model deepseek-v4-flash
```

`scripts/check-dispatch-policy.py` reads every brief against the switch, and
judges a brief by the mode the brief NAMES, never by today's switch position
(MEASURED 2026-08-14: five correct briefs went red at once when the switch
flipped, and the defect was in the checker, which now reads the named mode).
Legal head tokens: `opus`, `pi`, `codex`, and the emergency `fable`.

## The mode-switch checklist, from DD17's own row

Six steps, in order:

1. Set `VERSION_IN_FORCE` in `scripts/dispatch_policy.py`. Change NOTHING
   else in the tables.
2. Update the provenance: `SET_ON`, `SET_BY`, `REASON`, `REVERT_CONDITION`.
   Never invent a reason the owner did not give. Record the outgoing mode's
   reason, so the cancellation cannot be read as a judgement on a head.
3. Run `scripts/check-dispatch-policy.py` and EXPECT red on frozen briefs.
   A brief written under the old mode names that mode and WAS CORRECT. A
   frozen record is never rewritten, so red here is a defect in the GATE,
   never in the record.
4. If a mode is RENAMED, the retired name keeps resolving through `ALIASES`
   (C-41), because briefs carry it.
5. Delete or rewrite any memory or document that asserted the outgoing mode.
6. A running agent keeps the head that was correct when it was sent (owner,
   2026-08-14).

And know WHAT ACTUALLY CHANGES, which is more than the table: which code path
is live. An in-harness dispatch passes through NO tool, so under a mode that
leads in-harness the DD4 and DD18 refusals in `dispatch.py` never fire and
C-12's Agda slot accounting counts nothing. Under a mode that leads through
`herdr` they all fire again and `dispatch.py` becomes load-bearing: a defect
in it is invisible while nothing walks that path. Verify the path works
BEFORE the flip, not after.

## The dispatch commands

Everything is at `.claude/skills/codex-dispatch/dispatch.py`. Run it as
`.venv/bin/python`.

```sh
D=.claude/skills/codex-dispatch/dispatch.py
python3 $D check <brief>                       # validate WITHOUT launching. Use it every time.
python3 $D run <task> <brief> [--agda]         # launch now
python3 $D queue <task> <brief> [--agda]       # wait for a slot, then run, detached
python3 $D resume <task> --note "what survived" # continue a killed or exhausted agent
python3 $D wait                                # block until an agent returns, then report
python3 $D status                              # the census; it flags everything wrong
python3 $D gate-ready                          # exit 1 if a gate would read a half-written master
```

- **`check`** validates a brief without launching. It runs every refusal that
  `run` and `queue` run. Use it on every brief before dispatch.
- **`run`** launches now. Add `--wait` and run the whole command as a
  harness-tracked background job: the harness tracks THIS command, so its exit
  is the notification, and there is no separate arming step to forget.
- **`queue`** waits for a free Agda slot AND free write territory in a DETACHED
  process, then runs. It is safe to kill: the agent it launches is
  setsid'd/detached. Use it when slots or territory are held.
- **`resume <task> --note TEXT`** continues a killed or exhausted agent. The
  note tells the agent what survived so it does not redo it. It runs on the
  harness recorded at launch (MEASURED 2026-08-13: a resume on the wrong
  harness hands a pi session id to codex). A herdr resume works ONLY while the
  agent is still alive in its pane; after a clean finish the driver closes the
  pane, the agent is gone, and `resume` fails with `agent_not_found`
  (MEASURED 2026-08-13). Re-dispatch then.
- **`--agda`** declares the task will hold an Agda process, which C-12 caps.
  It also answers the model rule (`model_for`).
- **`--sandbox read-only`** for explorers whose deliverable is the final
  message. A brief that orders a written deliverable under read-only is
  REFUSED: the agent cannot write its own report.
- **`gate-ready`** refuses a full-tree gate while any agent is live: a gate
  then reads masters an agent is mid-write on, a spurious failure at best and
  a spurious GREEN at worst (MEASURED 2026-08-05).

Register the task row in `dev/PLAN.md` section 11 BEFORE dispatching
(section 6.0 rule 6), one row per code, 200-character cap, enforced by
`scripts/check-task-index.py`. The orchestrator got this backwards once and
had to fix it after.

## The refusals dispatch.py enforces

Every refusal exists because a failure actually happened. `check` and `run`
print the whole list. Each is a real gate:

| It refuses | Because |
|---|---|
| a brief not pinned in `agents/tasks/<CODE>/` (or legacy `_build/briefs/`) | the brief must survive a reboot and the owner must read what was asked (ORCHESTRATION section 3) |
| a brief with no `tier:` line | the head and the mode must leave a trace an audit can find |
| a brief with no `SCOPE (write)` section | the task kind is DERIVED from it; without it the rule bundle silently defaults to the smallest |
| a brief with no `RETURN` section | the deliverable's shape is what makes a return auditable |
| a brief with no checkable evidence demand (`file:line` or a URL) | a report without evidence can only be believed, never checked |
| a written deliverable under `--sandbox read-only`, or `--agda` under read-only | read-only blocks the agent's own report; Agda writes `.agdai` files |
| a brief with no ARCHIVE section and one with no LITERATURE section | DD18; one honest line naming the corpus satisfies either, on EVERY brief whatever the kind |
| a brief that does not carry DD4 | DD4 has no metric by ruling, so being stated IS its enforcement; `check-dd4-stated.py` reads the heading |
| a brief missing its kind's mandatory rule bundle | the kind is DERIVED from the write scope; run `python3 scripts/rules.py --for <kind>` and paste the bundle into SCOPE (read) |
| a task name with slashes or `..` | a task name becomes a filename and a registry key |
| a model the backend rejects | the agent dies in seconds having written nothing, which looks like a clean empty finish |
| a brief another LIVE agent already holds | two agents on one brief hold the same write territory |
| a live agent whose write scope intersects this brief's | two agents writing one file in one checkout see each other's edits |
| a task already running | a re-dispatch would double-launch |
| a third concurrent Agda agent | C-12's ceiling of two here; it says `queue` instead of waiting by hand |
| `wait --all` while more than one agent is live | it blocks until the SLOWEST finishes; every earlier return sits unnoticed behind it |
| a full-tree gate while any agent is live | the gate reads half-written masters |

## Knowing an agent finished. There is ONE procedure and it is not the waiter

**Use `run --wait` as a harness-tracked background job. One command per agent.**
The harness tracks THAT command, its exit IS the notification, one notification
per agent, and there is nothing to arm and nothing to re-arm.

```sh
# correct
python3 $D run LJ-1.200 agents/tasks/LJ-1-200/LJ-1.200.md --model M --wait   # backgrounded
```

**`queue --wait` DOES NOT WORK, and the trap is that it does not complain.**
`queue` ACCEPTS `--wait` in its `--help` and then IGNORES it: it detaches and
prints 「ARM THE NOTIFIER ... or pass --wait next time」, which is the advice you
already followed. **MEASURED 2026-08-14 by dispatching with it and watching the
command exit 0 in seconds while the agent ran on.**

**So `queue` ALWAYS needs a waiter armed after it.** Use `queue` only when a
slot is genuinely held, and arm the fallback in the same breath.

**AND THE WAY THIS SECTION WAS WRONG FOR AN HOUR IS THE LESSON.** It first
claimed both take `--wait`, marked MEASURED 「from their own `--help`」. **The
help text was measured; the BEHAVIOUR was not, and the claim was about
behaviour.** That is `load-bearing-claim`'s own rule broken inside the skill
that documents the tool: a claim written MEASURED names the search that
established it, and a `--help` listing does not establish what a flag does.

**MEASURED 2026-08-14, and it is why this section was rewritten:** the
orchestrator dispatched five agents with bare `queue`, armed waiters by hand,
and lost three returns. `status` was printing `!! 3 RETURN(S) NEVER REPORTED`
the whole time. The owner caught it, not the tool, and the tool had been
shouting.

**The requirement is absolute: you must know when an agent finishes, by
whatever means.** A dispatch whose completion you cannot observe is a dispatch
you did not make.

### The waiter, which is now the FALLBACK only

**Use it only to recover a dispatch already made without `--wait`.**

**One waiter reports once. That is the whole contract.** The tool says so in
those words. A waiter reports a single return and exits; every later return
sits unread. RE-ARM after every reported return.

- **`status` is the audit, not the notifier.** Run it whenever you are unsure
  and read it WHOLE: it names every return that was never reported.
- When several agents are live, use plain `wait`, never `wait --all`
  (refused with more than one live agent, MEASURED 2026-08-06: four returns
  lost behind one slow agent).
- A waiter whose stdout goes nowhere (started with `&` and output discarded)
  SILENTLY consumes the return it reports. The durable `returns.log` is the
  recovery: a lost notification is recoverable instead of gone.
- `status` prints loud banners: `!! N RETURN(S) NEVER REPORTED` and
  `!! NO WAITER IS ARMED`. **Never grep the status output down to the rows
  you expect**: that removes exactly the lines that are telling you
  something is wrong. Read the census whole.




## An agent that CREATES a herdr pane can be read as FINISHED. MEASURED 2026-08-14

**`[LJ-1.206]` was told to make a throwaway herdr agent as a safe test subject.
It created pane `w7:p16`, and while it was doing that the driver's stop loop
matched `--until idle` and declared the run finished: it read the pane, closed
it, and returned. The report was still a skeleton of PENDING sections.**

**The driver is TWO PHASE and that was not the failure.** It waits `--until
working` first and only then for a stop. **The failure is that an agent driving
herdr perturbs the very state herdr reports about IT.**

**So: never brief an agent to create or drive a herdr agent while it is itself
supervised by this driver.** A herdr-measuring task must either

- run OUTSIDE the driver, dispatched by hand and watched by a human, or
- measure by reading and by observing agents somebody else started.

**And the general form, which is worth more than the case:** a supervised agent
that manipulates its own supervisor's state machine will be misread by it.

## `herdr agent prompt` QUEUES. It does not interrupt. MEASURED 2026-08-14

**A prompt sent to a BUSY agent sits in a queue and is read only when the agent
finishes its current tool call.** The pane shows it as
`Steering: <your text>` with `Option+Up to edit all queued messages`.

**MEASURED, and it cost 4.25 hours.** An agent's `agda` invocation had run 3.06
hours. The orchestrator sent `herdr agent prompt` telling it to interrupt and
report the wall. **The message queued behind the very run it was telling the
agent to stop, and was never read.** An hour later the same run was at 4 hours
15 minutes and the instruction was still `Steering`.

**So: to reach a busy agent, you must first end what is keeping it busy.**

- **Kill the agent's OWN process, not its pane.** `ps -eo pid,etime,args | grep
  <the probe file>` finds it; `kill` on the Agda pid lets the tool call return,
  and the queued prompt is read within seconds. **MEASURED: the agent resumed
  and read the instruction immediately.**
- **Do NOT close the pane for this.** Closing kills the agent AND its context,
  irreversibly, when the only thing you needed to stop was one child process.
- **A prompt is not a stop.** It is a message for an agent that will get around
  to reading it.

**AND THE REAL CURE IS IN THE BRIEF, not in the pane.** Give every Agda brief a
WALL-CLOCK cap, so the agent stops itself: **a single `agda` invocation past 30
minutes is a wall, to be interrupted and reported with its elapsed seconds.**
The brief that lost 4.25 hours gave a heap cap and no clock cap.

## `blocked` is NOT finished, and the census is what stops it being forgotten

**RULED by the owner 2026-08-14, on `[LJ-1.205]`'s finding.**

herdr defines `blocked` as an approval or question UI awaiting input, and the
official skill says to inspect it and ANSWER it
(`.claude/skills/herdr/SKILL.md:58,154`).

**The driver used to wait `--until idle --until done --until blocked`, so an
agent that paused for approval read as FINISHED: the driver read the pane,
CLOSED it, and recorded a return.** Closing a pane is irreversible: the agent and
its context are gone. **That consequence was INFERRED from the code and never
measured**, and the owner ruled the safe side rather than waiting for it to
happen.

**Now:** `blocked` is not a stop state. The loop waits on `idle` and `done`
only, the pane stays OPEN, and the driver says which not-stopped it is, so a
pause for approval is never filed as a death.

**AND THE SECOND HALF IS THE MECHANISM, because the owner asked for it by
name:** review before closing is a human act, and a human act with no alarm is a
leak. **`status` now shouts about every blocked agent, prints the read and the
prompt command for each, and keeps shouting until the pane is gone.**

**So the rule for you:** a blocked agent is ALIVE and doing NOTHING. Read it,
answer it with `herdr agent prompt`, or stop it deliberately. **Never leave the
banner standing.**

## herdr, the pane server

`herdr` is a server that owns the terminal panes agents run in. The agent
survives the death of whatever launched it, and it is visible from any other
terminal. The default harness of `dispatch.py` is `herdr` (or `herdr-pi` for
a pi agent) and it follows the policy switch.

**The official skill `.claude/skills/herdr/SKILL.md` is the authority on
`herdr` the TOOL.** It regenerates from the binary (`herdr --skill`), so a
tool fact lives there and this section keeps only what Bedrock measured or
what `dispatch.py` does. The official skill's discovery doctrine is the
rule: the installed binary is the authority for command syntax, so print
`herdr --help` and the command group (`herdr agent`, `herdr pane`) instead
of trusting a snapshot (DOCUMENTED). It also requires `HERDR_ENV=1` before
herdr commands (DOCUMENTED); `dispatch.py` assumes a herdr-managed session
and does not check it (INFERRED from the driver).

**There is NO `agent stop` and NO `agent kill` subcommand.** MEASURED
2026-08-14 via `herdr agent --help`; the official skill documents no
`agent stop` or `agent kill` either (it documents `herdr server stop`
only as a prohibition, `.claude/skills/herdr/SKILL.md:193`), so the
finding stands. `herdr` owns the pane, so `pkill` reaches only the
launcher and never the agent (MEASURED, episode 2026-08-14: the agent
kept running AND kept its name, and the re-dispatch was refused because
the name was taken).

What is known to stop a herdr agent:

- **Close its pane**: `herdr agent get <name>` gives the `pane_id`;
  `herdr pane close <id>` kills the agent (MEASURED: agents die with their
  pane or workspace, observed 2026-08-13). This is irreversible: the agent
  and its context are gone, so you cannot resume it.
- **`send-keys C-c`**: UNMEASURED. Two `send-keys C-c` did not stop a
  working pi agent (episode 2026-08-14). The skill does not recommend it.
  The official skill documents logical keys and key validation; it never
  claims C-c stops an agent, so there is no contradiction (DOCUMENTED and
  UNMEASURED).

The honest moves when an agent must stop: let it finish and read its pane,
or `resume <task> --note` with what survived. A herdr resume works only
while the pane is alive.

Other herdr facts:

- **Names are lowercase.** `LJ-1.123` is refused; `herdr_name()` maps it to
  `lj-1-123`, the ONE place the mapping lives (MEASURED 2026-08-13). The
  official skill documents the name grammar `[a-z][a-z0-9_-]{0,31}` and
  that a name is cleared when its agent exits (DOCUMENTED); the two agree.
- **The wait is TWO PHASE, and it is Bedrock's own measurement.** The
  official skill documents the five lifecycle states `idle`, `working`,
  `blocked`, `done`, `unknown` and `--until` (DOCUMENTED; their meanings
  live there, not here). Its settled-state rule PREDICTS the measured
  at-once return: a bare wait or a one-phase `--until idle` returns AT
  ONCE, because the agent is already idle at submission (MEASURED
  2026-08-13, twice). So the driver waits for `working` FIRST, then for
  `idle`/`done`/`blocked` (the driver's own pattern, `dispatch.py` lines
  928 to 949).
- **`blocked` is an approval or question UI (DOCUMENTED), and the driver
  counts it as a stop (INFERRED).** The driver's stop loop is `--until
  idle --until done --until blocked`, so a pi agent that stops at an
  approval question reads as finished. The official skill says a blocked
  wait is a cue to inspect `agent get` and `agent read` and send input
  (DOCUMENTED). The two readings stand; the owner decides which Bedrock
  wants.
- **The driver never passes `--wait` to `agent prompt`.** The official
  skill documents `agent prompt --wait` (settled-state wait) and the
  `agent_prompt_stalled` five-second rule (DOCUMENTED). Bedrock sends the
  brief with a bare `agent prompt` and waits itself, so the stalled rule
  never fires on the driver's path (INFERRED).
- **The pane closes on a clean finish and STAYS on a death.** A dead
  agent's terminal is the only record of how it died. `status` reports
  panes kept as evidence and never sweeps them; `status --sweep-panes`
  closes only the stale clean ones. The official skill's "do not close
  what you did not create" rule is satisfied: the driver closes only the
  pane it split (DOCUMENTED and MEASURED).
- **A freshly split pane is not yet a shell.** `agent start` on a pane
  created milliseconds earlier fails with `agent_pane_busy`; the driver
  retries on a bounded loop (MEASURED 2026-08-13). The official skill
  documents the requirement: the pane must be at its interactive prompt
  with no foreground command, `agent start` never creates layout, and it
  defaults to a 30-second startup timeout (DOCUMENTED).
- **The workspace.** Agents share the project workspace with the owner
  (owner's instruction 2026-08-13). The workspace ID is the key
  (`.state/herdr-workspace`); the LABEL is a hint, never a key, because
  herdr relabels a workspace from its content (MEASURED 2026-08-13: a
  label lookup missed and a second workspace was created).
- **A herdr pane does not inherit the launcher's environment.** `GHCRTS`
  is handed over with `--env` on the split, only when the dispatch holds an
  Agda slot (MEASURED). The official skill says herdr injects the caller's
  CONTEXT (`HERDR_WORKSPACE_ID`, `HERDR_TAB_ID`, `HERDR_PANE_ID`); the two
  do not contradict, because context variables and the shell environment
  are different things (DOCUMENTED and MEASURED).
- **The model is passed through the `-- <AGENT_ARG>...` slot** for a codex
  agent, or `--provider deepseek --model <model>` for pi. `agent start`
  alone uses the agent's own default model and silently downgrades
  (MEASURED 2026-08-13). The official skill documents the `--` slot
  (DOCUMENTED); the exact args are Bedrock's instance.
- **Read a pane:** `herdr agent read <name> --source recent --lines 400`
  is the driver's final read. The official skill documents the read
  sources (`visible`, `recent`, `recent-unwrapped`, `detection`), `--format
  ansi`, the `--lines` limits, and the alternate-screen fallback: ask the
  agent to write its complete response to a file and read that
  (DOCUMENTED; they live there, not here). Bedrock reports are files in
  `agents/tasks/`, so the file fallback is already the norm.
- **Manual pane work.** The official skill's safety and coordination rules
  bind: `--no-focus` for background work, explicit pane IDs or `--current`
  (never the UI-focused pane), IDs parsed from JSON, nothing closed that
  the dispatch did not create, no `herdr server stop`, no killing the main
  Herdr process, and CLI server errors exit 1 while syntax errors exit 2
  (DOCUMENTED). The driver already follows them: explicit pane IDs from
  JSON and `--no-focus` on the split.

## The ten mistakes of 2026-08-14, and each one's rule

The orchestrator made all ten in one session. Each is stated with its lesson,
so the skill is the mistake and the cure together.

1. **`pkill` does not kill a herdr agent.** The agent kept running AND kept
   its name; the re-dispatch was refused (`agent_name_taken`); the
   orchestrator did not notice. There is no stop and no kill subcommand.
   `send-keys C-c` is UNMEASURED. Closing the pane works and is
   irreversible. **Check `herdr agent list` before re-dispatching a name.**
2. **A `RUNNING` row is not proof your launch worked.** After a re-dispatch
   the orchestrator read `RUNNING LJ-1.187` and concluded success; that row
   was the OLD agent, and the launch log's third line was the error. **After
   a dispatch, read the launch log, not the status table.**
3. **One waiter reports once.** The orchestrator armed one, it reported a
   single return and exited, and three later returns sat unread. **Re-arm
   after every reported return.**
4. **The tool shouts and the orchestrator filtered it out.** `status`
   prints `!! N RETURN(S) NEVER REPORTED` and `!! NO WAITER IS ARMED`, and
   `status | grep RUNNING` removes exactly those lines. **Never grep the
   status output down to the rows you expect.**
5. **A broken path stays green while nothing walks it.** `dispatch.py`
   refused EVERY brief since the briefs moved into `agents/tasks/`, because
   its pinning check still demanded `_build/briefs/`. Nobody saw it because
   the mode in force sent every default in-harness, and an in-harness
   dispatch never passes through the tool. **Verify the incoming path
   BEFORE changing which path is live.** (Both homes are now accepted;
   `agents/tasks/<CODE>/` is the RIGHT answer and it is tracked.)
6. **The refusal message cited the rule that contradicted it.** The refusal
   named `dev/ORCHESTRATION.md` section 3, and section 3 already read that a
   brief is pinned in `agents/tasks/<TASK>/`. The orchestrator believed the
   CODE over the RULE it cited and built a workaround in `_build/briefs/`,
   which `AGENTS.md` forbids: `_build/` is a temporary folder, not a rubbish
   bin. **When a tool and the rule it cites disagree, open the rule.**
7. **An in-harness dispatch bypasses every refusal.** It passes through no
   tool, so `dispatch.py`'s DD4 and DD18 gates do not fire and C-12's slot
   accounting counts nothing. **Under a mode that leads in-harness, those
   gates are review-only whether or not their rows say so.**
8. **Two agents in one cluster collide.** One found all four target masters
   dirty and its file RED, and stopped correctly. Another lost about fifteen
   minutes and said the cure: a brief line saying the sibling's file may be
   red, so build generic first, would have cost nothing. Two dispatches were
   also pointed at the SAME report path; the agent that arrived second
   refused to overwrite and wrote a new file, which was better than the
   plan. **In a cluster, name the sibling's possible redness in the brief.**
9. **The head and the model are two different choices.** The switch's
   `cases` table picks the HEAD; `--model` picks the model; `model_for()`
   rules the model. **A `tier:` line names the head, the mode, and the
   model when it is not the default.**
10. **Taking an exception's head without its conditions is invisible.** The
    orchestrator sent four adversarial reviews to `fable` because the owner
    had once named fable for one review. `check-dispatch-policy.py` passed
    all four, because DD17's emergency tier makes `fable` a legal token.
    DD0 and C-43 are the rules. **If the sentence justifying another head
    will not write, take the head the table gives.**

## The rules that bind the dispatcher, each at its home

**DD25, the adversarial rule** (`dev/PLAN.md`). A negative return is
adversarially reviewed at maximum effort, IMMEDIATELY, and the two are then
read together. The trigger: a refusal, a NO-GO, a RED gate, a stop taken as
the deliverable, a refutation of the brief's premise, or a landed result
that misses its band floor. The head comes from the switch's adversarial
row, never from a head written elsewhere. The index row for a negative
return must NAME its review's code, which is DD25's own declared
enforcement point and which had lapsed on every negative return for
twenty-odd dispatches. Operational form: `dev/ORCHESTRATION.md` section 1.

**DD0** (`dev/PLAN.md`, C-43). The owner's temporary instructions are not an
interpretation of the rules and not permission to disobey them. Never
derive a standing head choice from a one-off owner instruction. The test:
would the sentence justifying this choice write, citing the RULING rather
than the owner's past act? If not, take the head the table gives.

**The emergency tier** (DD17). It binds TWO conditions and the second is
scarce: a return that does not do its task well, AND a point where the
loop cannot advance (a gate whose verdict blocks the next funded build, a
wall no measured cure passes, a refutation that would re-price the route).
A stop, a refutation and a RED are GOOD returns and never a trigger. Each
use names its trigger in the brief and records the escalation.

**DD18** (`dev/PLAN.md`). Every brief carries an ARCHIVE section and a
LITERATURE section; every return carries ARCHIVE USED and LITERATURE USED
at `file:line`. `dispatch.py` REFUSES a brief missing either, on every
brief whatever the kind. The escape valve is one honest line naming the
corpus and saying nothing in it bears on the task. This binds the codex
path; an in-harness dispatch applies both by hand.

**DD4, and its only enforcement is repetition** (`dev/PLAN.md`). Maximize
the code the two proofs share, and write it generic. Stated in EVERY brief
whatever the kind, answered in every return. `scripts/check-dd4-stated.py`
gates that a brief SAYS it, reading the heading and never the content,
because DD4's row rules out a metric. This skill itself follows it: it
serves any task kind and any head, not the cases that bit one week, and the
mode tables above are written for BOTH modes for that reason.

**C-12, the Agda quota** (`dev/LESSONS.md`). One Agda process per agent,
`GHCRTS="-A64m -I0 -M8g"`, cap never raised, a heap exhaustion reported as
a wall. The quota is per-agent and not machine-wide (owner's correction,
2026-08-07), but a task that MEASURES check time gets a quiet machine, and
that is the orchestrator's to arrange: never write "confirm you are alone
before you measure" into a brief, because an agent inside the sandbox
cannot do it (ORCHESTRATION section 2.1, measured `[LJ-0.5]`).
`dispatch.py` implements the ceiling as TWO slots at `-M8g`, and refuses a
third (the ceiling is two, not four, because two `--profile=definitions`
runs corrupt each other's numbers; see the file's own comment).

**PLAN section 6.0.** Register the task row BEFORE dispatching, one row per
code, 200-character cap. An idle slot is a defect (DD17): at every return,
name what is unblocked and dispatch into the free slots.

## What an in-harness mode changes

An in-harness dispatch passes through no tool. It is an Opus subagent
inside the orchestrator's own session, which `dispatch.py` cannot start,
stop, or count. So under a mode whose default head is in-harness:

- `dispatch.py`'s refusals never fire: the DD4, ARCHIVE and LITERATURE
  gates are review-only, and the orchestrator writes them into the prompt
  by hand (ORCHESTRATION section 1.1, DD25).
- C-12's Agda slot accounting counts nothing: two in-harness agents can
  run Agda against a ceiling nobody measures.
- The switch still makes a wrong head DETECTABLE through the `tier:` line
  and `check-dispatch-policy.py`; it cannot make one impossible.

That is the honest limit the switch itself records: it drives the default
harness, the checker, and the inspection command, and nothing more.
